<WikiLayout title="WARDOGS Wiki Research Methodology" description="How WARDOGS Wiki verifies sources, handles version-sensitive mechanics, separates evidence from interpretation, and corrects records." canonicalPath="/methodology/" pageType="WebPage">
  <h1>WARDOGS Wiki Research Methodology</h1>
  <p>WARDOGS Wiki is maintained as a version-aware game reference. This methodology explains how information moves from a source into a guide or database record, how uncertainty is communicated, and where the limits of a claim are recorded.</p>

  <h2>Evidence levels</h2>
  <h3>Official documentation</h3>
  <p>Publisher and developer pages, patch notes, platform listings, and official announcements are the preferred sources for releases, named features, supported platforms, and documented rule changes. An official source establishes only what it actually states; it is not extended into an unsupported statistic or strategy claim.</p>
  <h3>Version-bound observation</h3>
  <p>Some mechanics are visible in the game but are not fully documented. These observations are tied to the recorded patch snapshot and are described conservatively. A value is not treated as permanent when an update could change it.</p>
  <h3>Strategic interpretation</h3>
  <p>Guides may explain practical decisions, trade-offs, and team roles. Strategy is labeled through its wording and never presented as an official guarantee. Recommendations are grounded in the described mechanic and include context that can change the outcome.</p>

  <h2>Verification workflow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the page subject, user question, and version-sensitive claims.</li>
    <li>Prefer first-party evidence and compare it with the current project snapshot.</li>
    <li>Separate confirmed facts from observed behavior and editorial analysis.</li>
    <li>Connect the record to relevant systems, guides, collections, and comparisons.</li>
    <li>Review the title, description, canonical path, visible heading, and source context before publication.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Unknown and disputed information</h2>
  <p>When a statistic or relationship cannot be verified responsibly, the Wiki uses <strong>Currently unconfirmed</strong> or omits the value. Conflicting reports are not resolved by guessing, averaging unsupported numbers, or copying an unattributed database.</p>

  <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
  <p>Pages carry a review date and version context. Material corrections are made in the source record so related database views and guides stay aligned. Stable URLs are preserved where possible. Readers can use the <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> to understand the correction standard and the <a href="/sources/">sources page</a> to see the evidence hierarchy.</p>

  <h2>Independence and commercial separation</h2>
  <p>Advertising does not determine coverage, rankings, or conclusions. Sponsored placement is not used as evidence. Generated decorative media, when present, is not represented as authentic gameplay or as proof of a mechanic.</p>
</WikiLayout>
